# Context

## Background
This commit lands the incident-driven fix batches that had
accumulated uncommitted in the tree since release 0.9.0:
- Pylemma bootstrap schema failure (2026-10-27): agents emitted
  task-level round fields and priorities 6-7; strict decoding
  rejected the whole backlog.
- Slit Codex account lapse (2026-10-30): a subscription expiry
  made every Codex call fail identically; the controller retried
  the final review three times and reported only
  "execution: exit status 1".
- Stackfall run analysis (2026-11): $162.71 run dominated by
  cache reads; T014 repeats caused by per-check worktree ids and
  CTest timings looking like new failures; import boundary skip
  between T025/T026; done tasks listed as attention.

Full findings, reproduction steps, and review history: NOTES.md
sections dated 2026-10-27 through 2026-11-03.

## Why This Way
- Repeat normalization is deliberately narrow (16-hex check ids
  with directory boundaries, trailing CTest durations only, and
  only inside observed output). An earlier broader version
  collided distinct commands and patch names; an adversarial
  review proved it and the narrowing is pinned by regressions.
- repair() hashes normalized evidence but passes raw feedback to
  agents, so artifact paths stay usable. Pinned by a Go test after
  a reviewer showed the suite passing with the raw call restored.
- Identity is checked before bootstrap can write: bootstrapping a
  different task-file selection would create a second backlog
  before the mismatch surfaced.

## Gotchas
- Prompt wording cannot repair saved plans, task commands, or
  explicit verification overrides; only new prompts change.
- Unrecognized unstable output still reaches the repair limit by
  design.
- Review agents with write access mutated the tree during the
  review; scope reviewers read-only before trusting a tree.

